---
// One idea: after the 10-year yield had RISEN over the prior year, the next
// twelve months were still usually fine — and barely different from the months
// after falls. The exhibit's job is to make the two bars look almost the same,
// because they are; the split that actually matters (inflation) gets its own
// surface further down, one idea per surface.
//
// Our computation. FRED GS10 (monthly-average 10-year constant maturity yield,
// from April 1953) joined with Shiller's US total-return series (Yale). Each
// month needs a look-back year and a look-forward year, which gives 819 usable
// start months, April 1954 to June 2022 — the series ends where Shiller's
// dividend column does. Forward return is the next 12 months, dividends
// reinvested, nominal.
//
// Identity colours, per the house rule: gold = months after a yield RISE,
// jade = months after a fall. The same mapping is reused by the 2x2 exhibit
// so the reader learns it once. Navy text on gold measures 6.6:1 at 13.5px,
// white on jade (#0B4732) 10.7:1 — both clear the 4.5 floor.
import ToolBrand from '../ToolBrand.astro';

const ROWS = [
  { key: 'up', label: 'the 10-year was higher than a year earlier', n: 434, median: 11.9, positive: 74 },
  { key: 'down', label: 'the 10-year was lower than a year earlier', n: 381, median: 13.6, positive: 80 },
];
const SCALE_MAX = 16; // % — headroom above the larger median so neither bar hits the wall
---

<div class="yn">
  <div class="yn-head">
    <span class="yn-lbl">Shares mostly rose anyway</span>
    <span class="yn-sub">What US shares returned over the next 12 months &middot; 819 monthly readings, Apr 1954 &ndash; Jun 2022</span>
  </div>

  <div class="yn-body">
    {ROWS.map((r) => (
      <div class="yn-grp">
        <div class="yn-grp-h">
          <span class="yn-cond"><i class={`yn-sw ${r.key}`}></i>{r.label}</span>
          <span class="yn-n">{r.n} months</span>
        </div>
        <div class="yn-track" role="img"
             aria-label={`After months where ${r.label}, the median next-12-month total return was ${r.median} per cent, and the return was positive ${r.positive} times in 100`}>
          <span class={`yn-bar ${r.key}`} style={`width:${(r.median / SCALE_MAX) * 100}%`}>
            <span class="yn-in">+{r.median}%</span>
          </span>
        </div>
        <p class="yn-foot">median next 12 months <b>+{r.median}%</b> &middot; positive <b>{r.positive} times in 100</b></p>
      </div>
    ))}
    <p class="yn-note-inline">
      Resampling the sample in three-year blocks, the two groups&rsquo; ranges overlap
      comfortably &mdash; the gap between them is inside the data&rsquo;s own uncertainty.
      What is not in doubt is the level: both groups spent most of their time going up.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="yn-out">
    <p class="yn-out-s">
      Whether the 10-year yield had <b>risen or fallen</b> over the past year told you
      almost nothing about the year ahead. The split that mattered was hiding in a
      different number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="yn-notes">
    <p>
      Yield change measured over the trailing 12 months, FRED GS10 monthly averages.
      Forward return is the following 12 months, dividends reinvested, before inflation,
      Shiller US data (Yale). Overlapping windows. Our computation.
    </p>
    <ToolBrand />
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
.yn{
  --yn-bd:var(--color-border); --yn-t1:var(--color-dark); --yn-t2:var(--color-body);
  --yn-gold:var(--color-gold); --yn-jade:var(--color-brand-700); --yn-deep:var(--color-navy);
  --yn-mono:var(--font-mono),'SF Mono',Menlo,Consolas,monospace;
  background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--yn-bd);border-radius:12px;
  box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,.05),0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.04);
  overflow:hidden;margin:26px 0;color:var(--yn-t1)
}
.yn-head{padding:14px 20px;background:var(--color-surface);border-bottom:1px solid var(--yn-bd)}
.yn-lbl{display:block;font-size:14.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.1px;color:var(--yn-t1)}
.yn-sub{display:block;margin-top:4px;font-size:11.5px;font-weight:600;
  text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.8px;color:var(--yn-t2)}
.yn-body{padding:4px 20px 14px}
.yn-grp{padding:15px 0 6px;border-bottom:1px dashed var(--yn-bd)}
.yn-grp:last-of-type{border-bottom:0}
.yn-grp-h{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;gap:10px;
  flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:9px}
.yn-cond{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;font-size:14.5px;font-weight:700;color:var(--yn-t1)}
.yn-sw{width:13px;height:13px;border-radius:3px;flex:none;display:inline-block}
.yn-sw.up{background:var(--yn-gold)}
.yn-sw.down{background:var(--yn-jade)}
.yn-n{font-family:var(--yn-mono);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--yn-t2)}
.yn-track{position:relative;width:100%;height:34px;border-radius:5px;overflow:hidden;
  border:1px solid var(--yn-bd);background:var(--color-surface)}
.yn-bar{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:flex-end;height:100%;min-width:0}
.yn-bar.up{background:var(--yn-gold)}
.yn-bar.down{background:var(--yn-jade)}
.yn-in{font-family:var(--yn-mono);font-size:13.5px;font-weight:700;padding:0 8px;white-space:nowrap}
/* Navy on gold 6.6:1, white on jade 10.7:1 — measured, both clear 4.5. */
.yn-bar.up .yn-in{color:var(--yn-deep)}
.yn-bar.down .yn-in{color:#fff}
.post-content .yn-foot{margin:9px 0 0;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.4;color:var(--yn-t2)}
.yn-foot b{font-family:var(--yn-mono);font-size:15px;font-weight:700;
  color:var(--color-brand-700);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
.post-content .yn-note-inline{margin:14px 0 0;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--yn-t2)}
.yn-out{background:var(--yn-deep);color:#fff;padding:17px 20px}
.post-content .yn-out-s{font-size:15px;line-height:1.62;color:#D8DEE8;margin:0}
.yn-out-s b{font-weight:600;color:#fff}
.yn-notes{padding:14px 20px 15px;border-top:1px solid var(--yn-bd);background:var(--color-surface)}
.post-content .yn-notes p{font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--color-muted);margin:0 0 8px}
</style>
